Q: Page Persistence After Reload in Electron App Development A: <p>The problem is that Electron always starts from your default page (Login) on reload, because it doesn't know where the user was. You need to persist the current page state manually.</p>
<p><strong>Option 1: Use <code>localStorage</code> (simplest approach)</strong></p>
<p>Save the current page whenever the user navigates:</p>
<pre><code>// After successful login, save state
localStorage.setItem('currentPage', 'home');
</code></pre>
<p>On app load / reload, read that value first:</p>
<pre><code>window.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () =&gt; {
  const page = localStorage.getItem('currentPage');
  if (page === 'home') {
    showHomePage();
  } else {
    showLoginPage();
  }
});
</code></pre>
<p>On logout, clear it:</p>
<pre><code>localStorage.removeItem('currentPage');
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Option 2: Use <code>electron-store</code> (recommended for production)</strong></p>
<p>Install it:</p>
<pre><code>npm install electron-store
</code></pre>
<p>In <code>main.js</code>:</p>
<pre><code>const Store = require('electron-store');
const store = new Store();

// Save on login
store.set('currentPage', 'home');

// Read on startup, default to 'login'
const lastPage = store.get('currentPage', 'login');
</code></pre>
<p>Then use <code>lastPage</code> to decide which HTML file or route to load in your <code>BrowserWindow</code> instead of hardcoding the login page.</p>
<p><strong>Key point:</strong> Make sure you are NOT unconditionally calling <code>win.loadFile('login.html')</code> every time the window is created. Check the persisted state first, then load the correct page.</p>

Q: Why is $(SolutionDir) undefined in dotnet CLI? A: <p>Perhaps use a helper and search for the .sln file i.e.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using System.IO;

public static class SolutionHelper
{
    public static string GetSolutionDirectory()
    {
        var directory = new DirectoryInfo(
            Path.GetDirectoryName(System.Reflection.Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly().Location));

        while (directory != null &amp;&amp; !directory.GetFiles(&quot;*.sln&quot;).Any())
        {
            directory = directory.Parent;
        }

        return directory?.FullName ?? throw new DirectoryNotFoundException(&quot;Solution file not found&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
